  "title": "Food",
  "description": "Learn essential food and drink vocabulary in Korean, plus how to say what you want, eat, and drink.",
  "guidebook": "## Talking About Food in Korean 🍚\n\nKorean has no separate word for \"a\" or \"the\" — context tells you which is meant. So **밥** (*bap*) can mean \"rice,\" \"the rice,\" or just \"a meal.\"\n\n**Saying what you want:** Use **~을/를 주세요** (*~eul/reul juseyo*), meaning \"please give me~\". Pick **을** after a consonant (밥을) and **를** after a vowel (커피를). Example: **커피를 주세요** — \"Coffee, please.\"\n\n**Saying you eat/drink:** Use **먹어요** (*meogeoyo*, \"eat\") and **마셔요** (*masyeoyo*, \"drink\"). Both end in **-어요**, the friendly present tense ending.\n\n- **저는 밥을 먹어요** — \"I eat rice.\"\n- **저는 물을 마셔요** — \"I drink water.\"\n\n**Topic marker:** **저는** (*jeoneun*) means \"as for me / I.\" The **는** highlights the topic of your sentence.\n\n**Quick tip:** 🥚 계란 and 달걀 both mean \"egg\" — 계란 is more common in everyday speech. Practice ordering: \"**저는 ___ 주세요**\" works for almost any food or drink!",
